course: world
lesson: 55
title: Electricity — circuits, switches and safety
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Electricity is a flow of tiny charged particles through a material. A battery pushes the electricity, and wires carry it along.
explain: Electricity only flows around a complete loop called a circuit: from one end of the battery, through the wires and a bulb, and back to the other end. If there is any gap, the flow stops and the bulb goes out.
explain: A switch is a gate in the circuit. When it is on, it closes the gap and the bulb lights; when it is off, it opens a gap and the flow stops.
explain: Conductors, such as copper and other metals, let electricity flow easily. Insulators, such as plastic, rubber, wood and glass, stop it — that is why wires are metal inside and covered in plastic.
explain: Mains electricity from wall sockets is far stronger than a small battery and can kill. Never poke things into sockets, never touch plugs with wet hands, and keep away from power lines and substations (fenced places full of big electrical equipment).
fact: Electricity flows only around a complete circuit.
fact: A gap anywhere in a circuit stops the electricity flowing.
fact: A switch opens and closes a gap in a circuit.
fact: A battery stores chemical energy and pushes electricity around a circuit.
fact: Metals such as copper, iron and aluminium are good conductors.
fact: Plastic, rubber, wood and glass are insulators.
fact: Water with impurities (tiny bits of other things mixed in it), like tap water, can conduct electricity, so water and electricity are dangerous together.
fact: Electric wires are made of metal inside with a plastic coating outside.
fact: Adding more batteries in a row makes a bulb glow brighter.
fact: Mains electricity is much stronger than a battery and can kill.
fact: Electricity turns into light, heat, movement and sound in our devices.
exercise: What is a circuit? | A complete loop that electricity can flow around | A single wire with one loose end | A battery on its own | A light bulb with no wires
exercise: A bulb is joined to a battery with one wire, but the second wire is missing. What happens? | The bulb does not light because the loop is broken | The bulb lights at half brightness | The bulb lights normally | The battery explodes
exercise: What does a switch do? | It opens or closes a gap | It makes the battery stronger | It stores electricity for later | It turns plastic into metal
exercise: Which material is a good conductor? | Copper | Rubber | Wood | Plastic
exercise: Why are electric wires covered in plastic? | Plastic is an insulator and keeps us safe | Plastic helps the electricity flow faster | Plastic makes the wire conduct better | Plastic stores extra electricity
exercise: Why must you never touch a plug with wet hands? | Water can conduct electricity into your body | Water makes the plug stop working forever | Wet hands make the plug rusty at once | Water turns the electricity into hot steam inside
exercise: What does a battery do in a circuit? | It pushes the electricity around | It stops the electricity flowing | It is only used as a switch | It turns light into electricity
exercise: How can you make a bulb in a simple circuit glow brighter? | Add another battery in a row | Use a longer wire covered in plastic | Open the switch to let more light out | Take out one of the two wires
exercise: Which of these would NOT complete a circuit if placed in a gap? | A plastic ruler | A steel key | A copper coin | An aluminium foil strip
exercise: Why is it dangerous to fly a kite near overhead power lines? | The lines carry very strong electricity that can kill | Kites make the lines stop working | Power lines pull kites with magnets | The wind is always stronger there
exercise: What does a toaster change electricity into? | Heat | Sound only | Magnetism only | Chemical energy
exercise: Why are the handles of electricians' screwdrivers made of rubber or plastic? | They are insulators | They conduct electricity better | They make the screwdriver lighter | They help the metal stay cold
exercise: A torch does not light when switched on. Which is a sensible first thing to check? | Whether the battery is flat or put in the wrong way | Whether the torch is the right colour | Whether the room is dark enough | Whether the torch is too heavy
exercise: What is mains electricity? | The strong electricity from wall sockets | The weak electricity of a small battery in a torch | Electricity made by your body | The electricity in a magnet
test: Tom builds a circuit with a battery, a bulb and wires, but uses a pencil's wooden body to join two wires. The bulb stays dark. Why? | Wood is an insulator and blocks the flow | Wood is a conductor but the pencil is too short | The pencil needs to be sharpened | The bulb only works with plastic
test: In a doorbell circuit, what does pressing the button do? | It closes the gap so the bell rings | It opens a gap in the wires so the bell rings | It charges the battery | It changes the bell into a light
test: Which is true? | Electricity needs a complete path to flow | Electricity flows best through rubber | A single battery is as dangerous as mains | Electricity can flow through a broken circuit
test: A child wants to test whether a spoon is metal or plastic using a battery, a bulb and wires. What will show it is metal? | The bulb lights when the spoon fills the gap | The bulb stays dark when the spoon fills the gap | The spoon gets cold | The battery gets lighter
test: A kettle cable has a crack and the copper inside shows. What should you do? | Stop using it and get it replaced | Wrap it in wet cloth and keep using it | Touch the copper to check it works | Use it only when your hands are wet
test: What would happen to the lights in a circuit if a wire broke in the middle? | They would go out | They would get brighter | They would keep shining as before | They would change colour
test: Why is a metal ladder dangerous near power lines but a dry wooden one less so? | Metal conducts electricity and dry wood does not easily | Wood is heavier than metal | Metal is an insulator | Wooden ladders store electricity
test: In which device is electricity mainly changed into movement? | An electric fan | A light bulb | A heater | A toaster
test: Two batteries are placed in a row in a torch instead of one. What happens to the bulb? | It glows brighter | It glows more dimly | It stops working | It turns into a switch
test: bulb won't light, wire's come off one end, why | The circuit has a gap, so electricity cannot flow | The battery has gone cold | The bulb always needs two batteries to light up at all | One wire is enough for a bulb
test: why are wires plastic on the outside | Plastic is an insulator that keeps the electricity in | Plastic makes the electricity faster | Metal would melt the plastic | It is only for the colours
test: my little bro says it's fine to touch a plug with wet hands. true? | No, water can conduct electricity and it is dangerous | Yes, water blocks electricity | Yes, wet hands are insulators | No, but only in the bath
test: Leo says a plastic spoon would make the circuit work. right? | No, plastic is an insulator and does not conduct | Yes, any spoon works | Yes, plastic is a very good conductor of electricity | No, only wooden spoons work
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition necessary for the flow of electricity? | The circuit must be complete, without any gap | The circuit must contain at least one gap | The wires must be coated externally in a layer of metal | The circuit must be immersed in water
exam: Identify, from the materials hereinafter enumerated, that which is designated a conductor of electricity. | Copper | Rubber | Plastic | Glass
exam: It is given that a second battery is added in a row to a circuit containing a single bulb. Determine the consequent effect upon the bulb. | The bulb shall glow more brightly | The bulb shall glow more dimly | The bulb shall be extinguished forthwith | The bulb shall remain wholly unaffected
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the construction of electric wires. | They consist of metal internally with a plastic coating externally | They consist of plastic internally with a metal coating externally | They consist of rubber throughout their entire length | They consist of glass internally with a wooden coating externally
